// KNOWN HOLE, NOT YET CLOSED — a cross-package METHOD call is not capability-checked.
//
// `sneak` declares no capabilities at all and calls `String::clone` and `String::drop`
// from `std`, both of which declare `with(Alloc, Unsafe)`. This program CHECKS CLEAN
// today. It is committed as a live reproducer, not as a passing test: the gate asserts
// that it is still accepted, so the day the hole closes the gate fails and says so.
//
// WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM THE TWO CLOSED CASES. The sibling-submodule repair fixed
// lookups within one compilation unit's module tree. Free functions imported from a
// dependency package ARE checked (`println` requiring Console is refused correctly), and
// METHODS defined in the same module ARE checked (`Box_shout` is refused correctly).
// Only the intersection — a method reached across a package boundary — falls through.
//
// It is also the reason the std capability declarations added alongside the repair look
// like they cost consumers nothing: they are enforced inside std and, for methods, not
// yet enforced outside it. Closing this is expected to be the larger change, because it
// reaches the resolve-side method signature path rather than one signature table.
mod known_hole_cross_package_method {
    fn sneak() -> u64 {
        let a: String = "hello";
        let b: String = a.clone();
        let n: u64 = b.len();
        a.drop();
        b.drop();
        return n;
    }

    fn main() with(Alloc) -> i64 {
        return sneak() as i64;
    }
}
